The Wayland stack of stage 11, as a prototype (PLAN.md, "Этап 11").

Each package is built by its own build system (meson, autotools) with the
port's cross toolchain, against a sysroot that is a copy of DESTDIR - to
find what it needs from the system and what it needs patched before it
goes into external/ with reachover Makefiles.

  cross-meecho.ini   meson cross file; sysroot ~/gfx-sysroot
  cc.sh              the cross compiler, with -pthread mapped to libmthread
                     (the port's GCC refuses -pthread on purpose), and
                     -luchar on every link
  build-stack.sh     sysroot | native | PKG... | all
  patches/PKG.patch  what each package needs on MEECHO, under __minix
                     (the tree's .gitignore has *.patch: git add -f)
  compat/            for the sysroot only, what the libc does not have:
                     <threads.h> and sem_* over libmthread (compat.c,
                     threads.h), <uchar.h> with UTF-8 conversions whatever
                     the locale (uchar.c, uchar.h)
  meechowm/          the compositor of 11.4: tinywl with MEECHO's screen
                     (fbout.c, the fb driver's scanout buffers) and input
                     (input.c, the input server's devices)

  test/wlhello.c     the smallest client: a window of one colour in a
                     memfd buffer, waits for the compositor's frame
  test/qemu-gfx.py   tinywl (headless backend, pixman) and wlhello on QEMU
  test/qemu-meechowm.py
                     meechowm on QEMU's fb (virtual=1), the screen read back
                     through /dev/fb0: window, cursor, foot, console back
  test/board-meechowm.sh
                     the same on the board, over ssh, left running for
                     whoever is at the monitor
  test/fcftprobe.c   what foot asks of fcft, alone, with fcft's debug log
  test/uchartest.sh  compat/uchar.c against glibc in C.UTF-8, on the host
  ../test/fb/fbart.c the screen (or an area of it) as text, from /dev/fb0

Sources (~/gfx-src, shallow clones of these tags):

  wayland 1.26.0             gitlab.freedesktop.org/wayland/wayland
  wayland-protocols 1.49     gitlab.freedesktop.org/wayland/wayland-protocols
  libffi v3.8.0              github.com/libffi/libffi
  pixman-0.46.4              gitlab.freedesktop.org/pixman/pixman
  libdrm-2.4.134             gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/drm
  xkbcommon-1.13.2           github.com/xkbcommon/libxkbcommon
  xkeyboard-config-2.48      gitlab.freedesktop.org/xkeyboard-config/xkeyboard-config
  wlroots 0.20.2             gitlab.freedesktop.org/wlroots/wlroots
  foot 1.28.0, fcft 3.3.3, tllist 1.1.0   codeberg.org/dnkl
  freetype VER-2-15-0        gitlab.freedesktop.org/freetype/freetype
  fontconfig 2.18.3          gitlab.freedesktop.org/fontconfig/fontconfig
  expat                      the tree's (external/mit/expat), built by hand
  freebsd-evdev              sys/dev/evdev/input-event-codes.h of FreeBSD
                             (BSD-2), as <linux/input-event-codes.h>

Host tools (WSL, apt): meson, ninja-build, autoconf, automake, libtool,
libtool-bin, libltdl-dev, bison, gperf, libexpat1-dev; wayland-scanner
1.26 is built from the wayland source ("build-stack.sh native") because
the distribution's 1.24 rejects the XML of wayland-protocols 1.49; meson
1.12 from a venv in ~/gfx-native (fontconfig wants 1.11).

All licences MIT or BSD-like (FreeType under its FTL, not the GPL);
nothing GPL is used.  The stands take the font from the host
(DejaVu Sans Mono, Bitstream Vera licence); it is not in the tree.
